---
import Callout from "~/components/illustration-kit/Callout.astro"
import IllustrationFigure from "~/components/illustration-kit/IllustrationFigure.astro"
import Panel from "~/components/illustration-kit/Panel.astro"
import SideBySide from "~/components/illustration-kit/SideBySide.astro"
---

<IllustrationFigure
  name="two-envelopes-paradox"
  lead="The same swap seems to have two different answers."
>
  <p class="text-base leading-normal">
    You picked one of two envelopes at random. One contains twice as much money as the other.
    You open yours and see £20.
  </p>

  <SideBySide>
    <Panel title="Your envelope" class="min-w-0">
      <p class="display mt-3 text-3xl sm:text-5xl">£20</p>
      <p class="mt-2 text-sm">Opened</p>
    </Panel>
    <Panel title="Other envelope" class="min-w-0">
      <p class="display mt-3 text-3xl sm:text-5xl">?</p>
      <p class="mt-2 text-sm">Still sealed</p>
    </Panel>
  </SideBySide>

  <p class="font-bold">Should you swap?</p>

  <SideBySide>
    <Panel title="Before looking" class="min-w-0">
      <p class="display mt-3 text-3xl">£0</p>
      <p class="mt-2 leading-normal">
        Neither envelope was favoured. Swapping had no average advantage.
      </p>
    </Panel>
    <Panel title="After looking" class="min-w-0">
      <p class="display mt-3 text-3xl">+£5</p>
      <p class="mt-2 leading-normal">
        If £10 and £40 are equally likely, swapping looks profitable.
      </p>
    </Panel>
  </SideBySide>

  <p class="text-base leading-normal">
    The tempting calculation: swapping could gain £20 or lose £10. Give each a 50% chance and
    the average gain is £5.
  </p>

  <Callout tone="accent">
    <strong>Here is the paradox.</strong> If that argument works whatever amount you open, why
    was there no advantage to swapping before you looked?
  </Callout>

  <Fragment slot="caption">
    The catch is the assumed 50/50 chance. The other envelope can hold double or half, but those
    amounts need not be equally likely after you see £20. The claim that they are equally likely
    for every possible amount is false.
  </Fragment>
</IllustrationFigure>
